1
00:00:07,293 --> 00:00:10,155
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I want to talk about the...

2
00:00:10,175 --> 00:00:17,740
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I want to talk about the Seleka Sutta, Majjhima Nikaya 8.

3
00:00:22,024 --> 00:00:29,269
[SPEAKER_01]: And this was sort of... it's still in line with the whole practice of non-conflict that we've been discussing lately.

4
00:00:30,950 --> 00:00:31,931
[SPEAKER_01]: The practice, really.

5
00:00:32,131 --> 00:00:33,672
[SPEAKER_01]: The practice of nibbāna.

6
00:00:35,404 --> 00:00:37,585
[SPEAKER_01]: The ultimate non-conflict.

7
00:00:40,607 --> 00:00:40,807
[SPEAKER_01]: And...

8
00:00:45,830 --> 00:00:53,535
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's called sālekha and translated as... usually translated as effacement.

9
00:00:55,333 --> 00:01:02,346
[SPEAKER_01]: That it's kind of a... which means like a wearing away of passions, the wearing away of evil things.

10
00:01:02,726 --> 00:01:03,388
[SPEAKER_00]: The unwholesome.

11
00:01:03,548 --> 00:01:05,091
[SPEAKER_01]: The unwholesome.

12
00:01:05,131 --> 00:01:05,732
[SPEAKER_01]: To get those away.

13
00:01:06,353 --> 00:01:06,553
[SPEAKER_01]: And...

14
00:01:09,842 --> 00:01:15,528
[SPEAKER_01]: It's translated in the Pali dictionary as, eh... austere penance.

15
00:01:16,349 --> 00:01:16,549
[SPEAKER_00]: Hmm.

16
00:01:17,430 --> 00:01:28,522
[SPEAKER_00]: I think, usually, it's connected with that view of, eh... self-mortification, whereby you purify yourself, you purify the unwholesome states of your mind by enduring certain physical hardships.

17
00:01:29,613 --> 00:01:38,196
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by going through certain ascetic practices, you get to erase the lust from your mind and so on.

18
00:01:38,216 --> 00:01:39,756
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the common view, common practice.

19
00:01:40,036 --> 00:01:40,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

20
00:01:41,957 --> 00:01:45,958
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, yeah, so the Jains, for example, they're one of the...

21
00:01:46,920 --> 00:01:52,183
[SPEAKER_01]: One of the practices is called salekhāna, which is the fasting to death.

22
00:01:52,603 --> 00:01:53,064
[SPEAKER_01]: Starving.

23
00:01:53,304 --> 00:01:53,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Starving yourself.

24
00:01:54,464 --> 00:01:59,067
[SPEAKER_01]: And by gradually starving yourself, you gradually... You starve, lust, aversion, delusion.

25
00:01:59,087 --> 00:02:00,168
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the idea behind it.

26
00:02:00,208 --> 00:02:01,528
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you end up in nibbāna.

27
00:02:01,628 --> 00:02:02,509
[SPEAKER_00]: In nibbāna, yeah.

28
00:02:03,463 --> 00:02:14,553
[SPEAKER_01]: And so is that whole, again, that whole idea of, I'll practice this thing, I'll do this thing, and it's going to result in purification of me.

29
00:02:15,453 --> 00:02:22,459
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I will, for example, be nice to others, and it's going to get rid of my ill will.

30
00:02:22,780 --> 00:02:26,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's going to result in freedom from aversion.

31
00:02:26,643 --> 00:02:26,923
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

32
00:02:28,585 --> 00:02:28,805
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

33
00:02:30,667 --> 00:02:37,671
[SPEAKER_01]: Although, kindness and compassionate acts are impressive, austere practices are impressive.

34
00:02:38,732 --> 00:02:38,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

35
00:02:38,952 --> 00:02:42,074
[SPEAKER_00]: What we're interested in... A degree of those is even necessary.

36
00:02:42,354 --> 00:02:42,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

37
00:02:43,074 --> 00:02:47,697
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like the sense restraint in virtue often goes hand in hand with austere practices.

38
00:02:48,517 --> 00:02:53,360
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's necessary as a basis, but it's not that which purifies you.

39
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:56,782
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that which enables you to understand what real purification is.

40
00:02:59,118 --> 00:03:01,860
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this Salekha is from the Buddha's...

41
00:03:02,081 --> 00:03:09,968
[SPEAKER_01]: He's explaining it, what Salekha is, from his point of view, so to speak.

42
00:03:13,911 --> 00:03:20,077
[SPEAKER_01]: And I won't read it word for word, just a few sections.

43
00:03:21,118 --> 00:03:24,401
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the monk Mahācunda asks the Buddha,

44
00:03:25,085 --> 00:03:33,969
[SPEAKER_01]: Venerable Sir, various various views arise in the world, associated either with self or in regard to the world.

45
00:03:34,749 --> 00:03:46,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, does the abandoning and relinquishing of those views come about in a bhikkhu who is attending only to the beginning of his meditation training?

46
00:03:47,015 --> 00:03:51,997
[SPEAKER_01]: So, anyway, the Buddha replies, as to those various views about self and world,

47
00:03:53,725 --> 00:04:14,440
[SPEAKER_01]: If the object in relation to which those views arise, which they underlie, and which they are exercised upon, is seen as it actually is, with proper wisdom, this is not mine, this I am not, this is not myself, then the abandoning and relinquishing of those views comes about.

48
00:04:16,841 --> 00:04:17,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Does that make sense?

49
00:04:18,462 --> 00:04:18,682
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

50
00:04:19,983 --> 00:04:22,025
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you will abandon your views,

51
00:04:24,220 --> 00:04:27,002
[SPEAKER_00]: If you understand what they depend on.

52
00:04:29,444 --> 00:04:35,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Not if you psychologize your way out of them, or if you meditate, if you concentrate your way out of them.

53
00:04:36,610 --> 00:04:42,154
[SPEAKER_00]: It's while those views are there, see, the object of those views, it's underlined by the views.

54
00:04:44,375 --> 00:04:47,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means that the underlying cause of your views

55
00:04:48,839 --> 00:04:53,102
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to this or that, is there simultaneously present with this or that?

56
00:04:53,862 --> 00:04:57,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, it would not be gripped by such and such view.

57
00:04:57,965 --> 00:05:05,930
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you cannot overcome that view simply by using that view to kind of work your way out of whatever you have a view about.

58
00:05:06,630 --> 00:05:10,172
[SPEAKER_00]: So, practically speaking, sensuality is a view, for example.

59
00:05:11,013 --> 00:05:13,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality is a view that whereby

60
00:05:15,802 --> 00:05:23,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Perversion is established of that which is fundamentally, naturally, inherently repulsive, you deem as attractive.

61
00:05:24,746 --> 00:05:25,786
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because of a view.

62
00:05:26,827 --> 00:05:31,288
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that view is in regard to attraction, in regard to the object of the senses.

63
00:05:32,669 --> 00:05:37,631
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can now say no to all of the object of the senses for the rest of your life.

64
00:05:37,651 --> 00:05:39,772
[SPEAKER_00]: That view will still remain unaffected.

65
00:05:41,517 --> 00:05:50,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Because in order to undo the view in regard to attractive and see this inherently non-attractive, you need to understand what that view is.

66
00:05:50,364 --> 00:05:58,711
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't just say no to the object of that view, which is sense object, nor can you just say yes to the object of that view, which is your senses.

67
00:05:59,651 --> 00:06:07,658
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you understand the necessary basis for the object of your view, that you want, that you desire towards, you will abandon the view.

68
00:06:08,843 --> 00:06:14,127
[SPEAKER_00]: And in this case, the necessary basis for anything attractive is non-attractive.

69
00:06:14,967 --> 00:06:16,128
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the necessary basis.

70
00:06:16,308 --> 00:06:20,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Repulsive is the necessary basis for any form of sensual desire.

71
00:06:21,652 --> 00:06:33,921
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you have a sensual desire towards somebody else without body of you, body of that person being a necessary basis for any manifestation of that desire?

72
00:06:34,502 --> 00:06:35,062
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

73
00:06:37,804 --> 00:06:49,249
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there is no sense desire that can arise apart from the body, the body is its necessary, presently enduring, simultaneously enduring basis for that desire, for that object of your view, that this is attractive.

74
00:06:51,970 --> 00:07:09,279
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, if you understand that your view that this is attractive is fully within the non-attractive, what you deem non-attractive, what you deem repulsive, but not before or after, but as a container of anything attractive and anything that you have desire towards, you will abandon any desire towards attractive.

75
00:07:09,719 --> 00:07:15,722
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you could see it basically it's within the nature of non-attractive which is repulsive, dangerous and so on.

76
00:07:19,918 --> 00:07:27,620
[SPEAKER_00]: But you will not erase that view, you will not efface it, simply by adhering to, I will always look only at the ugly.

77
00:07:28,760 --> 00:07:34,762
[SPEAKER_00]: For the rest of my life, I will not look at the object of beauty of any kind, sound, smell, taste, touch.

78
00:07:35,482 --> 00:07:49,085
[SPEAKER_00]: I will only endure pain, witness pain, and look at the pain, in hope that I will erase my, basically, view of desire towards what I deem attractive.

79
00:07:50,983 --> 00:07:53,384
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you will never erase that desire.

80
00:07:53,764 --> 00:07:56,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not rooted in the object of it.

81
00:07:56,204 --> 00:07:58,465
[SPEAKER_00]: That now you are either saying yes to or no to.

82
00:07:59,225 --> 00:08:00,885
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's rooted in understanding of that desire.

83
00:08:00,905 --> 00:08:08,947
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you want to erase the desire, you have to understand, basically, the presently enduring necessary condition for your view of attraction.

84
00:08:09,667 --> 00:08:13,968
[SPEAKER_00]: The presently enduring necessary condition for your view of repulsion and hatred.

85
00:08:16,578 --> 00:08:20,461
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you do, that's how you abandon it, without actually having to deal with the objects.

86
00:08:21,262 --> 00:08:22,083
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what Buddha said.

87
00:08:22,443 --> 00:08:25,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Even for Tathāgata, he sees beautiful and agreeable things in the world.

88
00:08:26,206 --> 00:08:32,852
[SPEAKER_00]: But what Tathāgata doesn't have is desire and lust in regard to them, because he has abandoned the view that those things are beautiful in that desirable sense.

89
00:08:39,126 --> 00:08:54,134
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the only effacement, the only starvation of greed, aversion, delusion that can happen, cannot occur through method, cannot occur through observance, cannot occur through any form of mechanical practice, meditation technique.

90
00:08:54,354 --> 00:08:57,995
[SPEAKER_00]: It can only occur through understanding greed, aversion, delusion.

91
00:08:58,015 --> 00:09:00,897
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it erases then, once and for all.

92
00:09:15,329 --> 00:09:22,712
[SPEAKER_01]: The next section of that sutta goes into the the eight attainments, so the jhānas and the higher states of mind.

93
00:09:24,072 --> 00:09:31,175
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha says that one who's practicing jhāna, all these other states,

94
00:09:32,842 --> 00:09:36,846
[SPEAKER_01]: He might think, I am abiding in that sālekha.

95
00:09:37,407 --> 00:09:39,269
[SPEAKER_00]: In the starvation of the unwholesome.

96
00:09:39,449 --> 00:09:40,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Erasing of...

97
00:09:40,650 --> 00:09:41,551
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm abiding in that.

98
00:09:42,212 --> 00:09:49,259
[SPEAKER_01]: But it is not these attainments, that jhāna, that is called that erasing, that sālekha.

99
00:09:50,481 --> 00:09:53,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Because jhāna does not necessarily mean understanding.

100
00:09:55,705 --> 00:09:59,572
[SPEAKER_00]: You might have understood things to the extent necessary for you to withdraw yourself from it.

101
00:09:59,873 --> 00:10:08,469
[SPEAKER_00]: Understood the extent of sensuality, understood the extent of any concern of emotional involvement with the world.

102
00:10:09,545 --> 00:10:17,448
[SPEAKER_00]: But jhāna will not provide you with a fundamental understanding of that sense of self and ownership, which is the root of all.

103
00:10:17,468 --> 00:10:20,010
[SPEAKER_00]: It's root of sensuality, root of anger, root of everything.

104
00:10:20,850 --> 00:10:27,393
[SPEAKER_00]: And without that effort to understand and without that understanding of the Buddha's teaching, even jhāna cannot provide you with that.

105
00:10:28,685 --> 00:10:42,764
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, if somebody, without any Buddhist help or instruction, develops jhāna, overcomes sensuality, overcomes everything, he'll have much greater chances of then practicing correct salekha by developing the right view.

106
00:10:45,124 --> 00:10:49,826
[SPEAKER_00]: But, it's not... the right view, it's not the automatic result of the jhāna.

107
00:10:50,246 --> 00:10:58,470
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why we said, the one who thinks, oh, I'm in the jhāna, and that's why I'm uprooting all of it, all greed, all aversion, all delusion, he's not correct.

108
00:10:59,050 --> 00:11:00,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Because jhānas will not uproot all of it.

109
00:11:00,951 --> 00:11:03,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's just... that's just called a pleasant abiding.

110
00:11:03,872 --> 00:11:11,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, noble ones just... Secluded from unwholesome states, secluded from worries, secluded from any hindrance, it's very a pleasant, peaceful abiding.

111
00:11:11,855 --> 00:11:13,216
[SPEAKER_00]: And, I mean, on...

112
00:11:14,792 --> 00:11:19,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Numerous occasions the Buddha just said that's the abiding you everybody should strive for.

113
00:11:20,013 --> 00:11:30,956
[SPEAKER_00]: But, again, in itself, it will not really result in that fundamental starvation of avijja, starvation of ignorance, that it cannot occur again afterwards.

114
00:11:35,118 --> 00:11:41,619
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is then salekha, according to the Buddha?

115
00:11:41,879 --> 00:11:43,600
[SPEAKER_01]: This is how it should be practiced by you.

116
00:11:45,112 --> 00:11:54,435
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is it then?

117
00:11:55,056 --> 00:11:55,856
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you starve?

118
00:11:57,016 --> 00:11:58,597
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the necessary basis for cruelty?

119
00:12:03,159 --> 00:12:04,439
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you have cruelty without your will?

120
00:12:05,736 --> 00:12:10,639
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the necessary basis for the view of cruelty is the ill-will.

121
00:12:11,480 --> 00:12:16,023
[SPEAKER_00]: The object of your cruelty, the necessary basis for it, is ill-will.

122
00:12:16,803 --> 00:12:30,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Unquestioned, ignored, unaware of ill-will, you then entertain the view towards the objects of cruelty, the view of cruelty, the view of harm, and so on.

123
00:12:31,292 --> 00:12:32,313
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you abandon it?

124
00:12:35,312 --> 00:12:37,933
[SPEAKER_00]: You abandon it by removing the necessary basis for it.

125
00:12:41,855 --> 00:12:44,816
[SPEAKER_00]: And where is then the ill-will, which is the necessary basis for cruelty?

126
00:12:45,696 --> 00:12:46,717
[SPEAKER_00]: Where do you find ill-will?

127
00:12:47,757 --> 00:12:54,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it just mysteriously floating somewhere in space and time in your mind and then occasionally pops up?

128
00:12:58,562 --> 00:13:00,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it perhaps always

129
00:13:01,718 --> 00:13:08,323
[SPEAKER_00]: You are inseparable from and manifest through your choices and your actions.

130
00:13:08,423 --> 00:13:11,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you have ill will without the intention of ill will?

131
00:13:13,448 --> 00:13:25,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you commit a bodily act of ill will, if you haven't chose to act out of ill will, towards ill will, for the purpose of ill will?

132
00:13:26,758 --> 00:13:28,860
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ill will is where your action is.

133
00:13:29,421 --> 00:13:30,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Action by body, by speech, by mind.

134
00:13:34,101 --> 00:13:35,462
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's where you should stop.

135
00:13:36,483 --> 00:13:37,884
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not, you're not doing anything?

136
00:13:38,464 --> 00:13:43,647
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not starving things randomly on account of a random observance, because some teacher told you to.

137
00:13:44,268 --> 00:13:53,574
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, like, they like to do these determination practices and, oh, it's, you know, for these three months, I will not eat with a spoon or something.

138
00:13:54,155 --> 00:13:55,636
[SPEAKER_00]: And you see no relation to anything.

139
00:13:55,676 --> 00:14:00,379
[SPEAKER_00]: You just assumed a random observance, that now you will adhere to with your life.

140
00:14:04,142 --> 00:14:06,348
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, you completely misplaced your practice.

141
00:14:07,070 --> 00:14:10,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Your determination, your observance is based on this, literally, this observable...

142
00:14:12,096 --> 00:14:21,560
[SPEAKER_00]: I will not use a spoon, or I will not eat sugar, or I will not do this, for no other reason that you can see that pertains to your understanding, or like removing greed, aversion, delusion.

143
00:14:22,180 --> 00:14:35,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Just through out of faith, out of blind kind of adherence to a method, or like a very common observance would be, I will sit, you know, in meditation posture for five hours every day, and just endure that pain, and

144
00:14:36,332 --> 00:14:36,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

145
00:14:37,593 --> 00:14:44,719
[SPEAKER_00]: How does that relate to you really understanding the necessary basis for your greed, the necessary basis for your lust, the necessary basis for your distraction?

146
00:14:45,379 --> 00:14:46,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, you have no idea.

147
00:14:46,480 --> 00:14:53,926
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're just literally adhering to this observance with wishful thinking that will somehow erase things that need to be erased.

148
00:14:53,966 --> 00:14:56,107
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's no different than self-mortification.

149
00:14:56,988 --> 00:15:01,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you don't go to the point of I refuse to breathe and starve myself to death, like giants would sometimes do.

150
00:15:03,193 --> 00:15:05,875
[SPEAKER_00]: It's equally ignorant as giving in to sensuality.

151
00:15:10,511 --> 00:15:20,476
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you cannot arrive at the correct practice of erasing ill-will, the necessary basis for cruelty, by mechanically adhering to a set of prescribed motions and methods and steps.

152
00:15:21,457 --> 00:15:21,637
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

153
00:15:21,697 --> 00:15:24,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's not where ill-will is.

154
00:15:24,659 --> 00:15:31,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Ill-will is in your motivation towards those motions and steps and acts you do by body, speech or mind.

155
00:15:31,682 --> 00:15:36,005
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's something you cannot have a prescribed template that will always point at.

156
00:15:36,870 --> 00:15:42,735
[SPEAKER_00]: That's something you always have to find yourself in and then see whether it's rooted in ill-will or non-ill-will.

157
00:15:44,216 --> 00:15:55,086
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you want to erase cruelty and anything unwholesome, you must not lose the sight of your motivation behind your own action by body, by speech and by mind, by thought.

158
00:15:55,787 --> 00:15:57,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, why am I thinking about this?

159
00:15:58,929 --> 00:16:01,952
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it because this doubt is so unpleasant I want to get rid of it?

160
00:16:04,258 --> 00:16:10,001
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, you have resistance and aversion towards the discomfort of not knowing enough of something.

161
00:16:11,041 --> 00:16:13,782
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's rooted in ill-will.

162
00:16:13,822 --> 00:16:17,384
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're entertaining your ill-will by trying to get rid of doubt in such sense.

163
00:16:20,906 --> 00:16:23,907
[SPEAKER_00]: Or same, like more obviously, sensuality.

164
00:16:24,663 --> 00:16:29,289
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensual thoughts might present themselves as a possibility, but you need to say yes to it.

165
00:16:29,349 --> 00:16:33,113
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said, you need to welcome it, entertain it, and accept it.

166
00:16:33,153 --> 00:16:35,075
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when those things are a problem.

167
00:16:35,616 --> 00:16:41,103
[SPEAKER_00]: And all those welcoming, entertaining, accepting are choices you make towards it, and then repeat them, and repeat them, and repeat them.

168
00:16:44,066 --> 00:16:44,446
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes,

169
00:16:45,594 --> 00:16:50,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Ill-will, lust, delusion, distraction, whichever way we translate it, needs to be erased.

170
00:16:51,458 --> 00:16:53,700
[SPEAKER_00]: But the only way to erase it is to see where it's rooted.

171
00:16:53,840 --> 00:16:56,662
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's always rooted in motivation behind your actions.

172
00:16:57,783 --> 00:17:06,069
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't make the effort to look at what is the current action, what is the prospect of this action I'm about to do, what is it rooted in?

173
00:17:07,310 --> 00:17:09,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I doing this out of desire or non-desire?

174
00:17:10,372 --> 00:17:12,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Out of ill-will, aversion or non-aversion?

175
00:17:13,567 --> 00:17:16,168
[SPEAKER_00]: So, those acts need to be erased.

176
00:17:16,668 --> 00:17:19,829
[SPEAKER_00]: The ones that you know are rooted in greed, aversion, delusion.

177
00:17:20,249 --> 00:17:20,789
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing else.

178
00:17:23,730 --> 00:17:29,992
[SPEAKER_00]: And this whole domain is invisible to people if they never start practicing that, like, mindfulness in the proper sense.

179
00:17:30,032 --> 00:17:30,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Wait.

180
00:17:31,013 --> 00:17:32,733
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the context of my present situation?

181
00:17:32,833 --> 00:17:33,634
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I doing this?

182
00:17:33,674 --> 00:17:34,434
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the purpose?

183
00:17:34,494 --> 00:17:36,715
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the... What feeling is this rooted in?

184
00:17:37,175 --> 00:17:40,776
[SPEAKER_00]: That is presently enduring while I want to do this or want to do that.

185
00:17:42,079 --> 00:17:50,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if only you can see the objects of your views, then you would be judging your views based on the objects, not based on the motivation behind.

186
00:17:51,202 --> 00:18:05,468
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, eh... talking, like, for you, for somebody, talking is always rooted in wanting to distract themselves from the unpleasant feeling of boredom, for example.

187
00:18:06,497 --> 00:18:12,980
[SPEAKER_00]: But they are unaware of the motivation behind the talking, the underlying cause for them wanting to talk and use it as a distraction.

188
00:18:13,381 --> 00:18:16,983
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all they see is distraction, talking.

189
00:18:17,023 --> 00:18:18,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Talking means distraction.

190
00:18:18,704 --> 00:18:19,864
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, that's their judgment.

191
00:18:19,884 --> 00:18:23,486
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when they see somebody else talking, ah, he's just distracting himself.

192
00:18:24,467 --> 00:18:30,510
[SPEAKER_00]: As if no talking can be rooted in anything else except distraction, because they don't ever see anything else in themselves.

193
00:18:31,624 --> 00:18:35,826
[SPEAKER_00]: But the reason they don't see it, it's not because it's not there, it's simply because they never make the effort to see it.

194
00:18:35,966 --> 00:18:41,808
[SPEAKER_00]: They remain adhering to talking, and then, consequently, their practice becomes about avoiding talking.

195
00:18:41,928 --> 00:18:45,410
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll be silent for the rest of my life, if you want to take that practice to the extreme.

196
00:18:45,850 --> 00:18:51,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why the Buddha said it's an ignorant, foolish thing to do, and actually made it a wrongdoing for monks to practice the vow of silence.

197
00:18:53,053 --> 00:18:55,114
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, literally, it means you think the problem is in talking.

198
00:18:58,155 --> 00:19:00,156
[SPEAKER_01]: You can see that there is ill will.

199
00:19:02,573 --> 00:19:02,693
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

200
00:19:02,853 --> 00:19:03,414
[SPEAKER_00]: Ill-will is there.

201
00:19:03,434 --> 00:19:04,454
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind of ill-will is there.

202
00:19:05,815 --> 00:19:06,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, what do you do?

203
00:19:06,856 --> 00:19:08,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you don't fuel it further.

204
00:19:09,577 --> 00:19:11,738
[SPEAKER_00]: By body, by speech or by mind.

205
00:19:12,499 --> 00:19:15,801
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you recognize ill-will, where it is.

206
00:19:16,121 --> 00:19:16,321
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

207
00:19:17,982 --> 00:19:19,043
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize you're estranged.

208
00:19:19,063 --> 00:19:19,983
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not doing anything.

209
00:19:20,043 --> 00:19:20,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

210
00:19:20,503 --> 00:19:21,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Physically.

211
00:19:21,564 --> 00:19:23,285
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you ask yourself, so what do I do next?

212
00:19:23,525 --> 00:19:25,346
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is the intention of ill-will.

213
00:19:25,446 --> 00:19:25,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

214
00:19:26,047 --> 00:19:28,128
[SPEAKER_01]: So, so how...

215
00:19:28,148 --> 00:19:31,710
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the way you regard that ill-will, that motivation.

216
00:19:33,106 --> 00:19:33,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

217
00:19:34,487 --> 00:19:38,190
[SPEAKER_00]: See, like, you can restrain, like, say, I will walk towards you.

218
00:19:38,550 --> 00:19:40,392
[SPEAKER_00]: You've done something upsetting.

219
00:19:40,412 --> 00:19:41,052
[SPEAKER_00]: That upsets me.

220
00:19:41,993 --> 00:19:44,895
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I restrain my body, so I don't physically harm you on account of it.

221
00:19:45,496 --> 00:19:48,558
[SPEAKER_00]: I restrain my speech, so I don't verbally abuse you on account of it.

222
00:19:49,199 --> 00:19:51,921
[SPEAKER_00]: I restrain my thoughts, so I don't wish you harm and cruelty.

223
00:19:52,642 --> 00:20:00,588
[SPEAKER_00]: But, see, now I still have the pressure to find what I need to do to get rid of the ill will.

224
00:20:01,818 --> 00:20:03,038
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, wait, why?

225
00:20:04,819 --> 00:20:09,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I assume that view that I must get rid of the ill will?

226
00:20:09,820 --> 00:20:10,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, because it's unpleasant.

227
00:20:11,780 --> 00:20:16,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, so in reality, all I'm doing is acting out of trying to avoid discomfort.

228
00:20:17,342 --> 00:20:22,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, I limited the extent of my act on account of the discomfort of you upsetting me.

229
00:20:23,691 --> 00:20:36,126
[SPEAKER_00]: The first limitation is, I don't do anything by body towards you, I don't do anything by speech towards you, I don't do anything by mind towards you, but I am doing something internally, trying to figure it out, so I don't endure it anymore.

230
00:20:36,547 --> 00:20:38,069
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you're subjected to it.

231
00:20:39,823 --> 00:20:43,365
[SPEAKER_01]: But you still want to get... you want to overcome ill-will.

232
00:20:43,405 --> 00:20:44,265
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to overcome...

233
00:20:44,285 --> 00:20:44,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

234
00:20:45,026 --> 00:20:47,667
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the general, overall value.

235
00:20:47,967 --> 00:20:55,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Ill-will is something to be overcome, but you will never overcome for ill-will if you keep craving against the discomfort of it.

236
00:20:55,330 --> 00:20:55,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

237
00:20:56,151 --> 00:20:57,271
[SPEAKER_00]: But now you understand that.

238
00:20:57,952 --> 00:21:00,413
[SPEAKER_00]: So you stop trying to get rid of the discomfort of ill-will.

239
00:21:00,633 --> 00:21:06,296
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you end up overcoming... You starve the ill-will by not providing fuel for it.

240
00:21:07,282 --> 00:21:13,225
[SPEAKER_00]: But that starvation can only happen on the level of the feeling of your discomfort towards ill-will, of ill-will.

241
00:21:13,605 --> 00:21:19,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Not as a level, not on the level of, you know, like reasoning and psychologizing and so on.

242
00:21:19,148 --> 00:21:23,050
[SPEAKER_00]: That can help you to find out what is it that you need to starve.

243
00:21:23,330 --> 00:21:31,454
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the only one thing you need to starve is your resistance towards uncomfortable feeling, full stop.

244
00:21:31,574 --> 00:21:33,855
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter on account of what.

245
00:21:34,295 --> 00:21:37,196
[SPEAKER_00]: Has that uncomfortable feeling arisen?

246
00:21:37,496 --> 00:21:38,137
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter.

247
00:21:38,757 --> 00:21:44,440
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is, whenever there is discomfort felt, I will not crave to get rid of it.

248
00:21:45,840 --> 00:21:49,922
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, indirectly, you then remove anything that can be felt.

249
00:21:51,467 --> 00:21:52,188
[SPEAKER_00]: Uncomfortably.

250
00:21:52,388 --> 00:21:55,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Ill will, upset, suffering, anything.

251
00:21:55,691 --> 00:21:58,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality, the first... You starve at the roots.

252
00:21:58,933 --> 00:22:00,595
[SPEAKER_00]: You starve the roots, basically.

253
00:22:01,275 --> 00:22:02,757
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the only way you can starve a thing.

254
00:22:02,777 --> 00:22:07,120
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't starve a tree by trimming in branches, because it will keep growing.

255
00:22:07,481 --> 00:22:17,009
[SPEAKER_00]: You can manage the tree by cutting its branches, but you don't want to manage, because the roots keep growing, which means the more and more, and you can't keep up with trying to trim it off.

256
00:22:18,643 --> 00:22:25,088
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you find a way of killing off the roots, as the Buddha said, then it's like a palm stump, left, cut over its root, dead.

257
00:22:27,010 --> 00:22:39,139
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they're saying, others will be cruel, but here, we'll practice non... Well, I won't be cruel.

258
00:22:39,680 --> 00:22:42,482
[SPEAKER_01]: But you cannot say that here there will be cruelty.

259
00:22:43,703 --> 00:22:44,984
[SPEAKER_01]: But I won't feed it.

260
00:22:45,024 --> 00:22:45,584
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, yes.

261
00:22:46,124 --> 00:22:47,845
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know, it's a deepening of that.

262
00:22:47,865 --> 00:22:49,166
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's the same principle.

263
00:22:50,047 --> 00:22:51,167
[SPEAKER_00]: So... Because that's what it means.

264
00:22:52,548 --> 00:22:57,771
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, as I said, you are not not cruel by not harming anybody physically.

265
00:22:58,571 --> 00:23:03,014
[SPEAKER_00]: You're simply not expressing your cruelty to the extent of physical action, and that's a necessary step.

266
00:23:03,114 --> 00:23:03,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

267
00:23:04,114 --> 00:23:08,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you're not expressing your cruelty to the level... on the level of verbal action, mental action, but you're still cruel.

268
00:23:09,778 --> 00:23:09,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

269
00:23:10,098 --> 00:23:15,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you still resist the uncomfort, the discomfort of whatever upset has been caused to you.

270
00:23:16,685 --> 00:23:21,570
[SPEAKER_00]: If you stop resisting the discomfort, there is no basis for the view of cruelty, view of harm.

271
00:23:21,650 --> 00:23:22,611
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just... it's removed.

272
00:23:24,293 --> 00:23:28,137
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's that simultaneous principle we keep talking about.

273
00:23:28,157 --> 00:23:31,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally, these roots are simultaneously present with your view of cruelty.

274
00:23:33,217 --> 00:23:36,561
[SPEAKER_00]: These roots are a necessary basis for cruelty.

275
00:23:36,961 --> 00:23:53,058
[SPEAKER_00]: The necessary basis for bodily act, mental act, verbal act, or restraining from bodily act, verbal act, mental act, the necessary basis is resisting the uncomfortable feeling of of this situation, which then manifests through cruelty and harm.

276
00:23:54,095 --> 00:24:07,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, I restrain manifestation of cruelty and harm through my physical action, verbal action, mental action, but for as long as I don't uproot my resistance, my craving against the uncomfortable feeling of any kind, I am liable to cruelty.

277
00:24:09,366 --> 00:24:16,151
[SPEAKER_00]: But if I uproot that resistance, I am incapable of cruelty, even if I don't think about my physical, verbal or mental actions anymore.

278
00:24:17,091 --> 00:24:19,133
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot possibly be rooted in cruelty.

279
00:24:19,930 --> 00:24:23,292
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why, again, every action is intention.

280
00:24:23,872 --> 00:24:26,674
[SPEAKER_00]: Not every action is what you do by body, speech or mind.

281
00:24:27,375 --> 00:24:33,919
[SPEAKER_00]: What you do by body, speech or mind, the karma, is so because of the intention that determines that karma.

282
00:24:33,939 --> 00:24:34,699
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what Buddha said.

283
00:24:34,879 --> 00:24:36,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Every action is intention.

284
00:24:40,751 --> 00:24:59,738
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there is no intention of resisting the discomfort, craving for more comfort on the level of your feeling, craving for indolence towards neither comfort nor discomfort, if there are no such intentions, there are no manifestations of view of sensuality, view of cruelty, view of ill will, view of anything, basically, discrepant with your existence.

285
00:25:01,199 --> 00:25:02,839
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the truth, Sālekha.

286
00:25:03,339 --> 00:25:09,962
[SPEAKER_00]: You have raised the necessary basins, the necessary framework for everything unwholesome, ever.

287
00:25:19,348 --> 00:25:28,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Others will kill, others will take what is not given, or be non... uncelibate, speak falsehood, malicious... Others will not be virtuous, basically.

288
00:25:29,092 --> 00:25:34,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will go against the virtue on the level of their body, let alone their speech and mind.

289
00:25:37,095 --> 00:25:38,036
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is about...

290
00:25:41,197 --> 00:25:41,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

291
00:25:41,457 --> 00:25:42,397
[SPEAKER_01]: You will not adhere.

292
00:25:42,817 --> 00:25:44,618
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot help but have views.

293
00:25:44,658 --> 00:25:50,019
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't help whether you choose to question it or choose to not question it.

294
00:25:50,039 --> 00:25:52,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Simply by not questioning your own views, you're adhering to them.

295
00:26:09,092 --> 00:26:13,515
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot say, oh, I will not hold to any views, because that is your view that you hold.

296
00:26:14,696 --> 00:26:20,021
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can recognize that you're bound to hold some view, and then question what you hold.

297
00:26:21,642 --> 00:26:24,004
[SPEAKER_00]: And thus, you will not be the one that adheres to the views.

298
00:26:24,384 --> 00:26:34,532
[SPEAKER_00]: You will not be the one that will have difficulty relinquishing the view, when you see it as unwholesome, rooted in unwholesome, rooted in a contradiction.

299
00:26:36,193 --> 00:26:37,034
[SPEAKER_01]: We spoke the other day,

300
00:26:43,533 --> 00:26:45,594
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I think that's... that connects basically to that.

301
00:26:46,774 --> 00:26:53,116
[SPEAKER_00]: That the root of the entitlement is the root of that not questioning your position, assuming you're taking it at face value, taking it for granted.

302
00:26:55,036 --> 00:27:00,798
[SPEAKER_00]: And then entitlement is the product of it, that manifests again through your actions, by body, by speech or by mind.

303
00:27:02,098 --> 00:27:05,699
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot be entitled if you don't act in an entitled manner.

304
00:27:08,964 --> 00:27:11,045
[SPEAKER_00]: By body, by speech or by your thoughts.

305
00:27:13,525 --> 00:27:14,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Thoughts of entitlement.

306
00:27:18,487 --> 00:27:24,468
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you know, the fundamental entitlement is the entitlement to life, health and youth.

307
00:27:25,229 --> 00:27:28,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Or intoxication with life, health and youth.

308
00:27:28,990 --> 00:27:31,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, everybody feels entitled to be alive.

309
00:27:33,011 --> 00:27:33,251
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

310
00:27:34,428 --> 00:27:35,989
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're not entitled to the next breath.

311
00:27:36,369 --> 00:27:37,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are you entitled to the next breath?

312
00:27:37,369 --> 00:27:37,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly.

313
00:27:37,729 --> 00:27:37,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

314
00:27:38,029 --> 00:27:40,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because you just never questioned that entitlement.

315
00:27:40,310 --> 00:27:40,650
[SPEAKER_00]: It's mine.

316
00:27:40,910 --> 00:27:41,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's mine.

317
00:27:42,371 --> 00:27:42,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it yours?

318
00:27:43,011 --> 00:27:44,432
[SPEAKER_00]: So, are you in control of it?

319
00:27:44,752 --> 00:27:46,493
[SPEAKER_00]: Does it mean you can prevent it from disappearing?

320
00:27:48,053 --> 00:27:48,413
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

321
00:27:48,753 --> 00:27:49,073
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

322
00:27:49,734 --> 00:27:58,137
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, that means your entitlement is just fully your view that you refuse to let go of, refuse to question, refuse to basically drill into.

323
00:28:03,200 --> 00:28:04,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Like entitlement to health.

324
00:28:04,742 --> 00:28:09,069
[SPEAKER_00]: You are healthy, but if that changes, you cannot do anything about it.

325
00:28:09,109 --> 00:28:13,756
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can you be entitled to health, when it's not even in your own control?

326
00:28:16,497 --> 00:28:19,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, that's a whole new layer now.

327
00:28:19,558 --> 00:28:20,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

328
00:28:20,298 --> 00:28:26,439
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when you add this entitlement to the behavior of others that you demand and you feel entitled to request and so on.

329
00:28:27,259 --> 00:28:40,262
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, that's another thing that it's sort of obviously explained in the suttas, whereby for an arahant who has removed any and every sense of entitlement, even if the highway robbers attack him and chop his limbs one by one,

330
00:28:41,385 --> 00:28:43,626
[SPEAKER_00]: So, others... going back a bit, others will be cruel, but I won't be cruel.

331
00:28:43,726 --> 00:28:45,126
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not others shouldn't be cruel.

332
00:29:04,969 --> 00:29:07,892
[SPEAKER_01]: At the same time, others shouldn't be cruel.

333
00:29:07,932 --> 00:29:09,094
[SPEAKER_01]: But others will be cruel.

334
00:29:09,434 --> 00:29:10,195
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you expect?

335
00:29:10,515 --> 00:29:11,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Others are bound to be cruel.

336
00:29:12,357 --> 00:29:13,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Because no more people...

337
00:29:13,118 --> 00:29:13,759
[SPEAKER_01]: They will be cruelty.

338
00:29:14,039 --> 00:29:17,423
[SPEAKER_00]: They will be cruelty, they will be greed, they will be aversion, they will be desire.

339
00:29:18,185 --> 00:29:25,812
[SPEAKER_00]: And you projecting that there must not be cruelty anywhere in the world, it's basically wishful thinking, product of your own entitlement.

340
00:29:26,592 --> 00:29:32,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, look, I found this great teaching of the Buddha, the dhamma, thus I'm entitled to demand everybody to follow it.

341
00:29:32,878 --> 00:29:34,459
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a quite a naive notion.

342
00:29:35,039 --> 00:29:35,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignorant notion.

343
00:29:36,741 --> 00:29:41,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Or I'm, on account of it, I demand a certain set of behavior by everybody, because that is the ultimate truth.

344
00:29:44,667 --> 00:29:46,289
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, your own entitlement speaking.

345
00:29:48,416 --> 00:30:07,475
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's so... You can't have the world, you know, go the way you want it to go.

346
00:30:08,356 --> 00:30:08,476
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

347
00:30:09,958 --> 00:30:11,519
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only reason you would have even such a

348
00:30:12,547 --> 00:30:17,250
[SPEAKER_00]: The inkling of such an expectation is because you're not questioning your own views and your own entitlement.

349
00:30:18,211 --> 00:30:18,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Entitlements.

350
00:30:28,118 --> 00:30:31,480
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this thing is about finding where the problem really is.

351
00:30:31,700 --> 00:30:31,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

352
00:30:31,960 --> 00:30:32,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Addressing that.

353
00:30:32,981 --> 00:30:33,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

354
00:30:33,822 --> 00:30:36,684
[SPEAKER_01]: And not expecting... Not trying to deal with it externally.

355
00:30:36,764 --> 00:30:39,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Not expecting others who are going to respect you.

356
00:30:40,306 --> 00:30:40,506
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

357
00:30:42,304 --> 00:30:44,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Do the same thing as you do... Yeah.

358
00:30:46,690 --> 00:30:47,911
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you must be also be okay with...

359
00:30:48,192 --> 00:30:49,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, find a way to be... Oh, yeah.

360
00:30:49,934 --> 00:30:51,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Expect that and be okay with it.

361
00:30:51,095 --> 00:30:51,376
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to.

362
00:30:51,536 --> 00:30:53,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's part of your own practice, correctly.

363
00:30:54,360 --> 00:30:55,181
[SPEAKER_00]: You must be okay.

364
00:30:56,703 --> 00:30:57,604
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, as the Buddha said...

365
00:31:00,084 --> 00:31:06,111
[SPEAKER_00]: The wise one who is practicing for enlightenment would be enduring the abuse and unpleasant words of others.

366
00:31:06,651 --> 00:31:15,160
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, you endure heat, cold, elements, wind, rain, everything else that you have to endure physically, you're not saying.

367
00:31:15,821 --> 00:31:23,386
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot, like literally, like even the most insane person would not have an entitlement to change the weather or to control the weather.

368
00:31:24,206 --> 00:31:28,449
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to become extremely rational, but even then, you realize it's kind of futile.

369
00:31:28,829 --> 00:31:33,292
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the same attitude, recognize the futility of trying to change it, is others.

370
00:31:34,432 --> 00:31:38,395
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of... No, they should not speak cruelly to me and abusively.

371
00:31:39,153 --> 00:31:39,293
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

372
00:31:39,633 --> 00:31:43,215
[SPEAKER_00]: I will not be cruel and abuse when this is encountered in my experience.

373
00:31:43,595 --> 00:31:48,857
[SPEAKER_00]: I will endure it on the same level I endure heat, cold, hunger, discomfort.

374
00:31:49,297 --> 00:31:50,837
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it is on the level of the elements.

375
00:31:51,038 --> 00:31:52,738
[SPEAKER_00]: Others are elements for you, basically.

376
00:31:54,019 --> 00:31:57,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Their behavior is neither your problem nor your responsibility.

377
00:31:58,292 --> 00:32:05,274
[SPEAKER_00]: And you, making it your problem or your responsibility, is you acting out of your own entitlement, which is rooted in you avoiding your own responsibility.

378
00:32:07,435 --> 00:32:14,157
[SPEAKER_01]: In fact, I see that in the danger of the monastic tradition.

379
00:32:15,552 --> 00:32:15,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

380
00:32:15,812 --> 00:32:16,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you know what I mean?

381
00:32:16,413 --> 00:32:16,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

382
00:32:16,813 --> 00:32:18,174
[SPEAKER_01]: The form that comes with it.

383
00:32:18,574 --> 00:32:21,236
[SPEAKER_01]: It sort of encourages entitlement.

384
00:32:21,817 --> 00:32:23,738
[SPEAKER_01]: I am a monk, and thus you must treat me this way.

385
00:32:23,778 --> 00:32:32,624
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't... it doesn't... that encouragement is actually rooted in something that even precedes the form and the tradition, monastic or otherwise.

386
00:32:32,664 --> 00:32:33,625
[SPEAKER_00]: It's rooted in virtue.

387
00:32:35,466 --> 00:32:44,173
[SPEAKER_00]: The actual virtue, sīla, that is necessary for uprooting entitlement or erasing greed, aversion, delusion, is the first fetter.

388
00:32:45,317 --> 00:32:46,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Sīla bhāt paramāsa.

389
00:32:47,038 --> 00:32:48,078
[SPEAKER_00]: We spoke about it before.

390
00:32:50,659 --> 00:32:54,581
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, oh, rites and rituals, that we all know are ridiculous and silly.

391
00:32:55,621 --> 00:32:57,822
[SPEAKER_00]: The word is actual virtue and duty.

392
00:32:58,982 --> 00:33:03,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Your sense of duty, your sense of virtue, has to be your fetter first.

393
00:33:04,945 --> 00:33:08,446
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you abandon the fetter without breaking your virtue, without abandoning your duty?

394
00:33:09,227 --> 00:33:12,848
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you abandon it by seeing where the view towards it is rooted at.

395
00:33:15,287 --> 00:33:26,056
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that whole tradition and form, and this is what we must do, and this is what everybody must do, and as a Buddhist you do this, it's all rooted in that same kind of non-negotiable nature of virtue.

396
00:33:28,818 --> 00:33:34,903
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you get monastic tradition, modern one, previous ones, the future ones, it's just going to be extension of that same thing.

397
00:33:35,804 --> 00:33:36,485
[SPEAKER_00]: But, actually,

398
00:33:38,651 --> 00:33:55,118
[SPEAKER_00]: If you start questioning, like, not questioning virtue by doing the opposite of virtue, but questioning the purpose of virtue while you're virtuous, that's already how you start seeing what is the necessary basis for maintenance of the factor of this is my duty, I must do this and I must not do that.

399
00:33:56,391 --> 00:33:56,811
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

400
00:33:56,831 --> 00:34:08,757
[SPEAKER_00]: If you reflect upon your virtue that has been developed beforehand, if you reflect on it correctly, the point will come where you get to understand that it's not about what I'm doing, which is I'm keeping this virtue.

401
00:34:09,797 --> 00:34:13,419
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... it's about what I'm not doing anymore.

402
00:34:13,459 --> 00:34:14,519
[SPEAKER_00]: What I will not do.

403
00:34:15,079 --> 00:34:16,440
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I don't care what I will do.

404
00:34:17,941 --> 00:34:20,802
[SPEAKER_00]: All I care about is things that I will refrain from doing.

405
00:34:22,083 --> 00:34:25,044
[SPEAKER_00]: And at that point, you refraining from doing,

406
00:34:26,715 --> 00:34:28,095
[SPEAKER_00]: It ceases to be your doing.

407
00:34:28,956 --> 00:34:31,577
[SPEAKER_00]: It just becomes something that holds you and maintains you.

408
00:34:32,697 --> 00:34:42,580
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that whole entitlement of, oh, it's a Buddhist tradition, this is what you must do, this is what you must say, this is your behavior, is the extension of the fetter of sīlabhāta paramāsa.

409
00:34:45,441 --> 00:34:47,002
[SPEAKER_00]: And sometimes people get the sense of it.

410
00:34:47,202 --> 00:34:49,243
[SPEAKER_00]: The duty of virtue is imposed on you.

411
00:34:49,283 --> 00:34:50,503
[SPEAKER_00]: And it is.

412
00:34:50,543 --> 00:34:53,644
[SPEAKER_00]: And people get the sense of it, but they don't know how to understand it.

413
00:34:53,664 --> 00:34:56,085
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead, they dismiss the virtue as a whole.

414
00:34:57,684 --> 00:35:01,232
[SPEAKER_00]: And then they try to argue that you don't need to keep the precepts in order to practice the dhamma.

415
00:35:01,913 --> 00:35:08,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they don't see any other way other than fetter when taken, and no fetter if I don't take it at all.

416
00:35:18,201 --> 00:35:21,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's what the śalekha is, you know, the austere panic.

417
00:35:21,023 --> 00:35:21,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

418
00:35:21,403 --> 00:35:22,084
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

419
00:35:22,224 --> 00:35:30,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if somebody thinks that you will arrive at arahantship by being virtuous, that's another wrong way of thinking you're going to experience that erasing of unwholesome.

420
00:35:31,530 --> 00:35:38,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Because your greed, aversion, delusion is not rooted in your acts of sensuality nor abstaining from sensuality.

421
00:35:39,095 --> 00:35:41,497
[SPEAKER_00]: It's rooted in your view of sensuality.

422
00:35:43,331 --> 00:35:46,252
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can abstain all you want for the rest of your life.

423
00:35:46,412 --> 00:35:51,695
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't actually make the effort to understand it, you won't be free from it.

424
00:35:52,275 --> 00:35:56,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, abstaining from sensuality is always better than non-abstaining, even if you don't know.

425
00:35:57,057 --> 00:36:11,323
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Buddha said, even if a monk doesn't get anywhere in his practice, doesn't develop any distinction, any knowledge, if he spends the rest of his monastic life crying in tears, in pain, of restraint, it's still better than non-restraint.

426
00:36:13,614 --> 00:36:18,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if none of his wrong views diminish on account of it, it's still better.

427
00:36:19,119 --> 00:36:22,383
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you don't want to practice just, oh, it's still better.

428
00:36:22,403 --> 00:36:26,346
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to practice to be free from view, because then restraint will cease to be unpleasant.

429
00:36:27,207 --> 00:36:32,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you'll be free from that fetter of needing to, like, restrain yourself and so on, needing to do it.

430
00:36:33,713 --> 00:36:37,437
[SPEAKER_01]: For example, that the practice of confessing,

431
00:36:38,770 --> 00:36:39,350
[SPEAKER_01]: What you've done.

432
00:36:39,710 --> 00:36:39,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

433
00:36:39,911 --> 00:36:40,971
[SPEAKER_00]: It does not purify you.

434
00:36:41,111 --> 00:36:41,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

435
00:36:41,731 --> 00:36:44,612
[SPEAKER_00]: It just basically manages you from eroding even further.

436
00:36:45,132 --> 00:36:46,813
[SPEAKER_00]: But it does not uproot the roots.

437
00:36:47,253 --> 00:36:47,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

438
00:36:47,813 --> 00:36:50,034
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what it's... in monastic life.

439
00:36:50,094 --> 00:36:50,674
[SPEAKER_01]: I know, I know.

440
00:36:50,974 --> 00:36:51,934
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what people say.

441
00:36:52,094 --> 00:36:53,095
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you apologize.

442
00:36:53,195 --> 00:36:54,935
[SPEAKER_00]: You go and apologize when you've done something wrong.

443
00:36:54,995 --> 00:36:56,156
[SPEAKER_00]: To erase all my wrongdoing.

444
00:36:56,176 --> 00:36:56,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

445
00:36:57,276 --> 00:36:59,438
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's now your gratuitous view.

446
00:37:00,098 --> 00:37:02,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Apology is good, if you've done something wrong.

447
00:37:02,921 --> 00:37:13,629
[SPEAKER_00]: It will, next time, it will basically allow you to catch yourself before you go that far, because it's unpleasant to sort of admit that you made a fool out of yourself and so on, but it can actually serve with a good purpose.

448
00:37:14,250 --> 00:37:20,535
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you then overdo that, in a sense of now you develop the view that apology purifies you.

449
00:37:21,777 --> 00:37:29,142
[SPEAKER_00]: By going through the motion of apology, I uproot the basically unwholesome that made me do such and such thing for which I have to apologize.

450
00:37:29,542 --> 00:37:37,608
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's no different than believing if I enter this holy river, my last aversion delusion will be washed away by this water that the Buddha mocked in the suttas.

451
00:37:39,670 --> 00:37:40,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, so then I should not apologize?

452
00:37:41,091 --> 00:37:44,153
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you should apologize, but don't entertain the views on account of it.

453
00:37:44,747 --> 00:37:50,789
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... that apology should be used so that you can... To help you, basically, next time, don't need to apologize.

454
00:37:51,149 --> 00:37:52,509
[SPEAKER_01]: So that you're aware of what you're doing.

455
00:37:52,589 --> 00:37:52,789
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

456
00:37:53,129 --> 00:37:58,771
[SPEAKER_00]: The more you are aware of what you're doing, the more you'll be then aware of why you are doing it.

457
00:37:59,891 --> 00:38:01,052
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where the roots are.

458
00:38:01,092 --> 00:38:02,032
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the problem is.

459
00:38:02,772 --> 00:38:03,272
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like...

460
00:38:03,292 --> 00:38:04,753
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like, oh, I should...

461
00:38:04,773 --> 00:38:05,033
[SPEAKER_00]: I should...

462
00:38:05,053 --> 00:38:06,013
[SPEAKER_00]: I know... Apologize.

463
00:38:06,813 --> 00:38:11,475
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, that you kind of kept that erosion, prevented it from going further,

464
00:38:12,428 --> 00:38:16,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, ask yourself, why did I end up in a position for which I had to apologize?

465
00:38:17,050 --> 00:38:18,591
[SPEAKER_00]: Where were my actions rooted?

466
00:38:18,891 --> 00:38:20,391
[SPEAKER_00]: Where was my motivation at?

467
00:38:21,432 --> 00:38:30,436
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, oh, then you actually, you will also realize on some occasion that, actually, even if others are upset, I have no need to apologize, no reason to apologize.

468
00:38:30,456 --> 00:38:35,838
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you know clearly, surely, confidently, they was rooted in non-greed, non-aversion and non-delusion.

469
00:38:37,199 --> 00:38:39,800
[SPEAKER_00]: And you know that because you would not mind apologizing.

470
00:38:41,241 --> 00:38:50,107
[SPEAKER_00]: But you want to recognize that, like, that wrongdoing, even in things that are actually wrong, by body, by speech or mind, it's not rooted in those things.

471
00:38:51,228 --> 00:38:53,470
[SPEAKER_00]: It's rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, underlying them.

472
00:38:53,890 --> 00:38:56,272
[SPEAKER_00]: It's rooted, basically, in your motivation for it.

473
00:38:58,353 --> 00:39:01,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, we spoke about it before, like, harsh, blunt speech.

474
00:39:02,316 --> 00:39:05,338
[SPEAKER_00]: For most people, oh, it's full of ill-will, full of passion.

475
00:39:06,399 --> 00:39:07,820
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's their own limited mind.

476
00:39:08,720 --> 00:39:15,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Has no other way of comprehending it, except the only way they can comprehend it for themselves, which is, whenever I raise my voice, it's because I'm angry.

477
00:39:15,565 --> 00:39:17,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, whoever ever raises their voice, they must be angry.

478
00:39:19,188 --> 00:39:25,072
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will never find out whether that's really the case or not, because you're unaware of your own motivation.

479
00:39:25,272 --> 00:39:32,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you say, wait a minute, I can raise my voice out of pure compassion, out of pure concern, because this person won't hear it otherwise.

480
00:39:36,306 --> 00:39:45,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, with the example of confession, a person might... Confession is a good thing, exposing the fault, but then you use that to hide the fault.

481
00:39:45,873 --> 00:39:46,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

482
00:39:46,353 --> 00:39:46,533
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

483
00:39:46,833 --> 00:39:48,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, oh, I just...

484
00:39:48,474 --> 00:39:48,875
[SPEAKER_00]: I clear it.

485
00:39:49,035 --> 00:39:50,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you feel like, oh, it's my duty to confess.

486
00:39:51,156 --> 00:39:51,316
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

487
00:39:51,737 --> 00:39:53,298
[SPEAKER_00]: And now that my duty ended there.

488
00:39:54,018 --> 00:39:54,159
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

489
00:39:54,239 --> 00:39:59,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Your duty is to uproot that because of which you ended up in a position that you have to confess things.

490
00:40:00,563 --> 00:40:02,965
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why there was that instance of that monk who came to Buddha and said,

491
00:40:03,550 --> 00:40:04,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I can't keep all these rules.

492
00:40:05,191 --> 00:40:06,973
[SPEAKER_00]: It was hundred and something at a time.

493
00:40:07,073 --> 00:40:08,013
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't keep all of this.

494
00:40:08,073 --> 00:40:08,734
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just too much.

495
00:40:09,154 --> 00:40:11,556
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to leave the monastic life.

496
00:40:11,576 --> 00:40:13,378
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha said, can you keep the three rules?

497
00:40:14,578 --> 00:40:16,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

498
00:40:16,100 --> 00:40:16,420
[SPEAKER_00]: I can.

499
00:40:16,440 --> 00:40:16,940
[SPEAKER_00]: That's easy.

500
00:40:17,180 --> 00:40:17,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

501
00:40:17,441 --> 00:40:19,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, don't do anything that you know it's rooted in greed.

502
00:40:20,283 --> 00:40:22,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't do anything that you know it's rooted in aversion.

503
00:40:22,204 --> 00:40:24,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't do anything that you know it's rooted in distraction.

504
00:40:25,267 --> 00:40:27,808
[SPEAKER_00]: And that monk became an arahant on account of the three rules.

505
00:40:28,269 --> 00:40:30,731
[SPEAKER_00]: The three proper erasers.

506
00:40:33,429 --> 00:40:39,190
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when people cannot see things on that level where they are rooted, that's when they need these rules to keep them in check.

507
00:40:39,790 --> 00:40:42,091
[SPEAKER_00]: But keeping the rules will not necessarily remove the roots.

508
00:40:42,291 --> 00:40:46,192
[SPEAKER_00]: It will just make them proliferate further, which is a necessary step for everybody who starts.

509
00:40:46,832 --> 00:40:50,893
[SPEAKER_00]: But in itself, it's still rooted in your greed, aversion, delusion.

510
00:40:51,413 --> 00:40:52,713
[SPEAKER_00]: In itself, it's still a factor.

511
00:40:56,994 --> 00:40:59,695
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's part of the sutta.

512
00:41:00,095 --> 00:41:00,435
[SPEAKER_01]: This is...

513
00:41:02,855 --> 00:41:07,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Just as there is an uneven path, there is an alternative even path.

514
00:41:10,546 --> 00:41:14,212
[SPEAKER_01]: For the one who is cruel, there is the alternative non-cruelty.

515
00:41:16,460 --> 00:41:20,282
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a person given to cruelty has no cruelty to which to avoid it.

516
00:41:21,162 --> 00:41:22,583
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is cruelty.

517
00:41:22,823 --> 00:41:23,464
[SPEAKER_01]: I noticed that.

518
00:41:23,644 --> 00:41:24,704
[SPEAKER_01]: I see that within myself.

519
00:41:25,545 --> 00:41:26,285
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I have an option.

520
00:41:26,585 --> 00:41:27,145
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always an option.

521
00:41:27,185 --> 00:41:28,426
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what... that's exactly what it means.

522
00:41:28,586 --> 00:41:32,948
[SPEAKER_00]: There is always an option because cruelty is not rooted in set of circumstances.

523
00:41:33,509 --> 00:41:42,873
[SPEAKER_00]: If cruelty was really rooted in what you said to me, there is no option than to follow a certain set of behavior towards what you said.

524
00:41:43,374 --> 00:41:43,894
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not.

525
00:41:44,385 --> 00:41:49,528
[SPEAKER_00]: The cruelty is rooted in my choice to accept that as a reason or my choice to not accept that as a reason.

526
00:41:49,968 --> 00:41:54,030
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, cruelty is rooted in my choice to be cruel or to be not cruel.

527
00:41:54,930 --> 00:41:56,891
[SPEAKER_00]: To be sensual or to be not sensual.

528
00:41:57,712 --> 00:42:06,076
[SPEAKER_01]: So, could you also say that if you see the motivation of cruelty, that there is another phenomenon there?

529
00:42:06,156 --> 00:42:10,318
[SPEAKER_00]: If you see the motivation of cruelty, you see your responsibility for cruelty.

530
00:42:11,339 --> 00:42:13,560
[SPEAKER_00]: That means you see your choice for non-cruelty.

531
00:42:14,730 --> 00:42:19,214
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're able to see the other phenomenon of non-cruelty.

532
00:42:19,775 --> 00:42:22,077
[SPEAKER_00]: The option of non-cruelty becomes apparent.

533
00:42:25,180 --> 00:42:26,041
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can choose that.

534
00:42:26,801 --> 00:42:27,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

535
00:42:27,162 --> 00:42:27,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's an option.

536
00:42:29,744 --> 00:42:32,126
[SPEAKER_00]: While the other option remains there.

537
00:42:32,146 --> 00:42:33,467
[SPEAKER_00]: While the option of cruelty remains there.

538
00:42:33,487 --> 00:42:33,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

539
00:42:34,490 --> 00:42:35,751
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't take away the...

540
00:42:35,791 --> 00:42:36,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

541
00:42:36,151 --> 00:42:40,553
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your option of non-cruelty is not rooted in removing the option of cruelty.

542
00:42:41,033 --> 00:42:46,736
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's rooted in not entertaining it, as the Buddha said, not welcoming it, not delighting in it, not acting out of it.

543
00:42:47,297 --> 00:42:50,718
[SPEAKER_00]: By not doing these things, you are choosing, then, the option of non-cruelty.

544
00:42:53,520 --> 00:43:01,864
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're... you need to discern that option of non-cruelty and... On account of your... on account of discerning your responsibility for cruelty.

545
00:43:03,161 --> 00:43:06,744
[SPEAKER_00]: Your responsibility for your will, your responsibility for sensuality.

546
00:43:07,285 --> 00:43:09,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality is not in the beautiful things in the world.

547
00:43:11,148 --> 00:43:13,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Aversion, it's not in ugly things in the world.

548
00:43:16,373 --> 00:43:17,094
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in your choice.

549
00:43:21,798 --> 00:43:23,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, every action is intention.

550
00:43:32,820 --> 00:43:34,101
[SPEAKER_01]: And the sutta ends.

551
00:43:35,643 --> 00:43:40,228
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a bit more to the sutta, obviously, but it ends in this way.

552
00:43:40,248 --> 00:43:46,775
[SPEAKER_01]: It says that one who is himself sinking in the mud should pull out another who is sinking in the mud.

553
00:43:47,015 --> 00:43:48,276
[SPEAKER_01]: Such thing is impossible.

554
00:43:48,296 --> 00:43:48,937
[SPEAKER_01]: The one who is

555
00:43:51,305 --> 00:43:57,747
[SPEAKER_01]: That one who is himself untamed, undisciplined, with defilements, unextinguished, should tame another?

556
00:43:59,168 --> 00:43:59,808
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

557
00:43:59,948 --> 00:44:01,809
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible.

558
00:44:01,849 --> 00:44:04,050
[SPEAKER_00]: It's what we spoke about for all this time.

559
00:44:04,230 --> 00:44:09,952
[SPEAKER_00]: So, imagine, you're completely unaware of the sphere of your motivations.

560
00:44:10,792 --> 00:44:12,373
[SPEAKER_00]: That that's where the problems are rooted.

561
00:44:12,393 --> 00:44:14,453
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where lust is rooted, where aversion is rooted.

562
00:44:14,974 --> 00:44:15,894
[SPEAKER_00]: You're completely unaware.

563
00:44:15,954 --> 00:44:18,795
[SPEAKER_00]: You have absolutely no recognition within yourself

564
00:44:20,128 --> 00:44:24,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Towards craving, towards pleasure, craving against the pain, craving towards distraction of neutral.

565
00:44:25,010 --> 00:44:25,890
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't see any root.

566
00:44:25,910 --> 00:44:29,212
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't see the existence of roots or where those roots would exist.

567
00:44:29,852 --> 00:44:35,854
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you teach somebody that will... your teaching would result in them seeing those roots, seeing how to overcome them.

568
00:44:36,475 --> 00:44:37,775
[SPEAKER_00]: That is impossible.

569
00:44:39,216 --> 00:44:41,877
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot accidentally stumble upon dhamma.

570
00:44:42,497 --> 00:44:46,479
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, for example, like trying to make people do the right thing.

571
00:44:46,919 --> 00:44:47,159
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

572
00:44:47,775 --> 00:44:48,256
[SPEAKER_01]: You're cruel.

573
00:44:48,476 --> 00:44:49,317
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, well, that's wrong.

574
00:44:49,477 --> 00:44:51,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I'll tell you how you should be instead.

575
00:44:51,921 --> 00:45:00,492
[SPEAKER_00]: You should do this, but... You might be right, in terms of teaching them non-cruelty, but you are right for completely wrong reasons.

576
00:45:02,585 --> 00:45:06,947
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not right, because you also see why non-cruelty can result in uprooting the roots of cruelty.

577
00:45:07,107 --> 00:45:07,747
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't see that.

578
00:45:08,307 --> 00:45:13,769
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just whatever external value you ended up circumstantially adopting, which doesn't like cruelty.

579
00:45:14,370 --> 00:45:14,710
[SPEAKER_00]: It's good.

580
00:45:15,550 --> 00:45:19,111
[SPEAKER_00]: It's better than cruelty, but in itself, it will not free you from the roots of cruelty.

581
00:45:19,131 --> 00:45:22,153
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha said, even the good karma is still a karma.

582
00:45:23,933 --> 00:45:25,774
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot free you from karma altogether.

583
00:45:26,879 --> 00:45:31,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the roots of those actions, the motivation of those actions remains non-understood.

584
00:45:31,980 --> 00:45:45,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Also, when you are not free from that stuff, those unwholesome behaviors, motivations, complaining that others are, it's just a whole another level of ridicule.

585
00:45:45,684 --> 00:45:46,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it is, it is.

586
00:45:46,724 --> 00:45:47,625
[SPEAKER_00]: It's too far removed.

587
00:45:48,485 --> 00:45:50,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that shouldn't be like that.

588
00:45:50,105 --> 00:45:52,486
[SPEAKER_01]: He shouldn't speak like that to me or...

589
00:45:53,613 --> 00:45:58,157
[SPEAKER_00]: You might not help but have such thoughts, but you can certainly help yourself from expressing them.

590
00:45:59,438 --> 00:46:04,943
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you're able to help yourself from expressing them, then you'll be able to help yourself from indulging them mentally.

591
00:46:05,543 --> 00:46:09,146
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're able to help yourself not indulge them mentally, you'll be able to understand its roots.

592
00:46:10,027 --> 00:46:13,390
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Buddha compared to... it was a simile for sensualities.

593
00:46:13,430 --> 00:46:18,594
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's like a man in the water, trying to light... finds the two sticks in the water.

594
00:46:18,634 --> 00:46:21,216
[SPEAKER_00]: He's in the water, sticks in the water, he tries to light the fire with them.

595
00:46:21,256 --> 00:46:22,137
[SPEAKER_00]: Such thing is impossible.

596
00:46:23,018 --> 00:46:26,020
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, the man realizes, well, I can't light this fire, because the un-wet sticks are wet.

597
00:46:26,040 --> 00:46:27,040
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he comes out on the shore.

598
00:46:27,641 --> 00:46:29,502
[SPEAKER_00]: He dries, but the sticks are still not quite dry.

599
00:46:29,742 --> 00:46:30,562
[SPEAKER_00]: He tries to light the fire.

600
00:46:30,662 --> 00:46:32,183
[SPEAKER_00]: They won't, because they're still wet.

601
00:46:33,344 --> 00:46:38,547
[SPEAKER_00]: But if he patiently keeps them, maintains them, withdrawn from the water, they will dry out.

602
00:46:39,187 --> 00:46:40,248
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, he can light the fire.

603
00:46:40,928 --> 00:46:48,773
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the Buddha said, so it is for samanas and brāhmaṇas, who might live withdrawn physically from sensuality, physically from unwholesome states,

604
00:46:50,187 --> 00:46:54,449
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, they stepped out of the water, but mentally, they haven't withdrawn themselves from it.

605
00:46:54,909 --> 00:46:56,150
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, the sticks are still wet.

606
00:46:56,910 --> 00:46:58,691
[SPEAKER_00]: They will not light the fire of that knowledge.

607
00:47:00,111 --> 00:47:10,396
[SPEAKER_00]: But, those Samādhi Brāhmaṇas who are withdrawn physically, and then mentally, they recognize also their involvement, so they stop fueling that involvement, then the sticks will dry out, and then they can light the fire.

608
00:47:14,548 --> 00:47:20,271
[SPEAKER_00]: So, of course, you cannot have sticks dry on the shore while you're in the water.

609
00:47:21,372 --> 00:47:26,354
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can certainly not be in the water and still have the sticks wet.

610
00:47:26,394 --> 00:47:28,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, you haven't dried them properly.

611
00:47:28,235 --> 00:47:29,336
[SPEAKER_00]: You haven't put them in the sun.

612
00:47:30,076 --> 00:47:32,577
[SPEAKER_00]: You're keeping them covered in the shade or bury them in the soil.

613
00:47:32,697 --> 00:47:38,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Or trying to sort someone else's... Well, that's all the result of this basic principle that's on the level of your own mind.

614
00:47:40,842 --> 00:47:41,262
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a...

615
00:47:43,994 --> 00:47:54,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Another sutta, which is similar, says, there are these ten ways of subduing hatred, which there are just a single one.

616
00:47:56,244 --> 00:48:00,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking, he has done me harm, but what should I expect?

617
00:48:02,409 --> 00:48:03,550
[SPEAKER_01]: One subdues hatred.

618
00:48:04,471 --> 00:48:09,635
[SPEAKER_00]: What could you possibly expect from the realm, from saṃsāra?

619
00:48:10,240 --> 00:48:15,621
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not in control over your own breathing and heartbeat and everything else.

620
00:48:15,721 --> 00:48:18,482
[SPEAKER_00]: What level of insanity is required?

621
00:48:18,502 --> 00:48:23,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Irrational kind of basis is required for you to expect others to not do certain things.

622
00:48:24,903 --> 00:48:30,424
[SPEAKER_00]: It's, it's... Once you start realize that, oh, it's actually, it's actually inconceivable for me to expect that.

623
00:48:30,924 --> 00:48:34,625
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason it wasn't inconceivable is because you kept conceiving it through ignoring it.

624
00:48:35,165 --> 00:48:38,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Through that self-basically inflated sense of entitlement.

625
00:48:40,451 --> 00:48:44,026
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're just expecting it is not going to help at all.

626
00:48:44,883 --> 00:48:49,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the only reason the expectation is there, because the basis for such irrational expectation is there.

627
00:48:50,606 --> 00:48:54,688
[SPEAKER_00]: The basis that sort of is not seen correctly.

628
00:48:55,049 --> 00:48:57,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can't control elements, you can't control your own body.

629
00:48:58,250 --> 00:49:02,413
[SPEAKER_00]: What a silly thing is to keep expecting to control behavior of others, or speech of others.

630
00:49:02,953 --> 00:49:04,454
[SPEAKER_00]: And what a futile thing it is.

631
00:49:04,474 --> 00:49:07,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Because then you realize, and why would that matter what others do or say?

632
00:49:07,996 --> 00:49:10,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's not where my suffering is rooted to begin with.

633
00:49:11,677 --> 00:49:21,413
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason it matters is because you still blame THAT to be your suffering, and THAT, all that blame, is already rooted in your own entitlement, which is caused by your own ignoring, your own responsibility, and your own choices.

634
00:49:36,698 --> 00:49:39,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it says, he's doing me harm, but what should I expect?

635
00:49:40,521 --> 00:49:42,503
[SPEAKER_01]: He's going to do me harm, but what should I expect?

636
00:49:42,843 --> 00:49:43,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

637
00:49:43,764 --> 00:49:47,126
[SPEAKER_00]: It's similar, I think, it's in, in, eh, Thera Therigata, when...

638
00:49:47,246 --> 00:49:47,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

639
00:49:47,807 --> 00:49:52,010
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one doesn't get roped up over impossibilities.

640
00:49:52,490 --> 00:49:54,132
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible, yeah, exactly.

641
00:49:54,952 --> 00:50:02,158
[SPEAKER_00]: It was similar, I think, in Thera Therigata, when, eh, that monk or a nun said,

642
00:50:04,702 --> 00:50:04,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, no.

643
00:50:04,922 --> 00:50:06,924
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a monk who said that to a grieving mother.

644
00:50:07,524 --> 00:50:11,047
[SPEAKER_00]: Mother lost a child.

645
00:50:11,548 --> 00:50:13,449
[SPEAKER_00]: Or she was grieving that she might lose him.

646
00:50:13,490 --> 00:50:14,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe it was his mother.

647
00:50:14,330 --> 00:50:14,691
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't remember.

648
00:50:14,711 --> 00:50:22,257
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point was, if you stop and think about it, you don't know where that being came from, you don't know where that being is heading.

649
00:50:23,858 --> 00:50:27,301
[SPEAKER_00]: What possibility there is to grieve over anything, literally?

650
00:50:27,822 --> 00:50:29,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Because grieving implies

651
00:50:30,115 --> 00:50:35,240
[SPEAKER_00]: A certain degree of control, where it came from and went away, was taken from you, that which was yours.

652
00:50:35,740 --> 00:50:40,985
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you regard that which is yours, when you don't have a clue how it came to be and where it's heading?

653
00:50:41,386 --> 00:50:46,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally, absolutely, it's... you cannot even think it on a level of fantasy.

654
00:50:46,891 --> 00:50:52,896
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're honest to yourself, you realize, oh, this is utterly inconceivable, inaccessible to me.

655
00:50:53,757 --> 00:50:55,479
[SPEAKER_00]: So, grief becomes impossible.

656
00:51:00,975 --> 00:51:02,916
[SPEAKER_00]: How things came to be.

657
00:51:03,336 --> 00:51:04,597
[SPEAKER_00]: How a being came to be.

658
00:51:08,038 --> 00:51:10,259
[SPEAKER_00]: And where that being, what happens to it when it's not?

659
00:51:11,439 --> 00:51:12,579
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just utterly inconceivable.

660
00:51:12,679 --> 00:51:14,560
[SPEAKER_00]: You can choose a set of beliefs.

661
00:51:14,600 --> 00:51:16,241
[SPEAKER_00]: This is what happens after somebody dies.

662
00:51:16,541 --> 00:51:20,502
[SPEAKER_00]: This is what happens when, you know, they die like this or like that.

663
00:51:20,522 --> 00:51:25,904
[SPEAKER_00]: But all of that is something which means you cannot possibly know or verify that, because that's all secondary to your own.

664
00:51:26,804 --> 00:51:28,625
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know where you came from and where you're heading.

665
00:51:30,508 --> 00:51:35,352
[SPEAKER_00]: So, grieving on that external level, or blaming people on that external level, becomes inconceivable.

666
00:51:38,654 --> 00:51:41,497
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's pretty much the state of an anāgāmī.

667
00:51:41,777 --> 00:51:44,779
[SPEAKER_00]: He cannot possibly be attracted nor repelled by anything in the world.

668
00:51:45,060 --> 00:51:46,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he's sorted out his problem.

669
00:51:46,521 --> 00:51:52,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he has undone that entitlement which is the definition of existence.

670
00:51:52,525 --> 00:51:54,207
[SPEAKER_00]: Any existence is a degree of entitlement.

671
00:51:54,247 --> 00:51:59,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Any bhāva means there is an extent of entitlement for life, for health, for youth.

672
00:52:00,194 --> 00:52:04,497
[SPEAKER_00]: For aggregates, you remove that.

673
00:52:05,057 --> 00:52:15,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it becomes even more inconceivable to extend that entitlement in that further irrational manner of your entitled-to-demand behavior of other sets of aggregates that you cannot even fathom.

